      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we need to complete the square for both the \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) terms.\n\nFirst, consider the \\( x \\)-terms:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x \\]\nTo complete the square, we add and subtract \\((\\frac{6}{2})^2 = 9\\):\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9 \\]\n\nNext, consider the \\( y \\)-terms:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y \\]\nTo complete the square, we first factor out the 2:\n\\[ 2(y^2 - 2y) \\]\nWe then add and subtract \\((\\frac{-2}{2})^2 = 1\\):\n\\[ y^2 - 2y = (y - 1)^2 - 1 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 \\]\n\nNow, substitute these completed squares back into the original expression for \\( z \\):\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22 \\]\nSimplify the constant terms:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11 \\]\n\nThe expression \\((x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2\\) is a sum of squares, which is always non-negative and achieves its minimum value of 0 when \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\). Therefore, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is:\n\\[ z = 0 + 0 + 11 = 11 \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
