      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we need to complete the square for both the \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) terms.\n\nFirst, consider the \\( x \\)-terms: \\( x^2 + 6x \\).\nTo complete the square, we take half of the coefficient of \\( x \\), which is 6, divide by 2 to get 3, and then square it to get 9. Adding and subtracting 9 inside the function gives:\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9.\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the \\( y \\)-terms: \\( 2y^2 - 4y \\).\nTo complete the square, we factor out the 2 from the \\( y \\)-terms, so we get:\n\\[\n2(y^2 - 2y).\n\\]\nNow, we take half of the coefficient of \\( y \\) inside the parentheses, which is \\(-2\\), divide by 2 to get \\(-1\\), and then square it to get 1. Adding and subtracting 1 inside the parentheses gives:\n\\[\n2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2.\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these completed squares back into the original function:\n\\[\nz = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22.\n\\]\nSimplifying the constant terms, we get:\n\\[\nz = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11.\n\\]\nThe expression \\((x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2\\) is a sum of two squares, each of which is non-negative and achieves its minimum value of 0 when \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) respectively. Therefore, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is:\n\\[\n0 + 0 + 11 = 11.\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
